  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe last of the very light rain showers associated with the passage \nof a weak shortwave rolling through the area has ended early this \nmorning as high pressure ridging continues to deteriorate ahead of a \n997mb low over eastern Colorado lifting over the Plains today. The \nlow is expected to bring its warm front and associated rain showers \noverhead by this afternoon. With better WAA moving overhead today, \nexpect temperatures to once again climb into the upper 50s to mid \n60s across the area (even near the Great Lakes) by this afternoon. \nWhile there won't be much instability to work with when compared to \nthe summer months, given the robust isentropic lift and \nfrontogenesis seen with the warm front, the couple hundred Joules of \nMUCAPE will be enough to get rain showers and maybe a non-severe \nthunderstorm or two going across the area this afternoon and beyond; \nsome of the showers and storms could be heavy at times, with the \nlatest NBM showing a 50 to 90% chance of over 1\" of rain falling \nbetween this evening and Wednesday evening (the highest chances over \nthe south central and eastern U.P.). It does look like with some of \nthe CAMs that there may be a cessation in the rainfall late tonight \ninto Tuesday morning as the warm front stalls out, before another \nround of showers and potentially a few storms move back through the \nrest of the day Tuesday into the middle of the week. \n\nModel guidance has converged on the low slowing down over the \nNorthern Plains as it trudges east into the Great Lakes region this \nweek. With guidance keeping us under the warm sector of the low \nthrough Wednesday, we can expect rain shower and thunderstorm \nchances to at least continue on-and-off (worst/best-case being near-\ncontinuous) through the first half of the work week. Thus, rainfall \namounts could be notably higher than currently predicted in the \nforecast (0.75 to 1.50 inches between this morning to Wednesday \nevening). As the low continues to move east for the latter half of \nthe week, guidance is in fairly strong agreement that the low will \nsplit; the more northerly low going into northern Quebec whereas a \nremnant low looks to remain over the Great Lakes region until early \nFriday. This remnant low remaining over the Great Lakes looks to \nbring lake enhanced and eventually pure lake effect precipitation \nover the north to northeast wind belts early Thursday into early \nFriday over the U.P. as CAA works from north to south over our area. \nWhile mainly rainfall is expected with the lake induced \nprecipitation, as temperatures cool throughout the atmospheric \nprofile late this work week we may see a mix over to snowfall at \ntimes; no notable snowfall accumulations are expected at this time. \nAhead of the CAA, expect well above normal temperatures to continue \nover the U.P., with highs in the upper 50s to 60s continuing through \neven Wednesday (and lows only in the 50s through Tuesday night). \nHowever, once the CAA from Canada drops down, expect temperatures \n(particularly the highs) to lower closer to normal for early \nNovember, with highs only projected to get into the 40s to lower 50s \nand lows into the 30s to potentially 20s in the interior areas. \n\nExpect drier weather to return this next weekend as high pressure \nridging moves back in behind the lake effect precipitation. While \ntemperatures are looking to not nearly be as warm as they are \nexpected to be over the next couple of days, above normal high \ntemperatures are predicted to once again return for the end of the \nforecast period.",
